4 Gradient Descent with Momentum
We now add momentum. Its most widely used parameterization is \[
    \begin{aligned}
        \mathbf{v}^{\text{cl}}_{n+1}
            &= m^{\text{cl}}\, \mathbf{v}^{\text{cl}}_{n}
            - \lambda^{\text{cl}}\, b^{-1} \sum_{i:~ t(i) = n} \mathbf{g}_i(\boldsymbol\theta_n) \\
        \boldsymbol\theta_{n+1} &=
            \boldsymbol\theta_{n} + \mathbf{v}^{\text{cl}}_{n+1}
    \end{aligned},\quad
    0 \leq m^{\text{cl}} &lt;1, \quad
    \lambda^{\text{cl}} &gt; 0
    \label{eq:sgd-mom-classic}
\]
The parameter \(\mathbf{v}^{\text{cl}}\) has a well-known physical reading. Take \(n\) for a discrete time, and \(\mathbf{v}^{\text{cl}}\) is a velocity in the space of weights, with the batch-averaged gradient as the acceleration. We use a different aspect of momentum here.
The exponential moving average for the sequence \(\{g_n\}_{n=0,1,2,\dotso}\) is a sequence \(\{s_n\}_{n=0,1,2,\dotso}\) defined by the recursion \[
    s_{n+1} = m\, s_n + (1 - m)\, g_n, \quad 0 \leq m &lt; 1
\] with some initial condition for \(s_0\) that we are not interested in. Unrolling it, \[
    s_{n+1} = (1 - m)\sum_{k=0,1,2,\dotso} \, m^k\, g_{n-k}.
\] So momentum smooths the gradients of previous steps. Instead of using only the gradients at the current weight \(\boldsymbol\theta_n\), we sum the gradients taken at all earlier weights \(\boldsymbol\theta_{n-1}\), \(\boldsymbol\theta_{n-2}\), \(\boldsymbol\theta_{n-3}\), and so on, with the decreasing coefficients \((1-m)m\), \((1-m)m^2\), \((1-m)m^3\). The coefficients sum to one: \[
    (1-m)\sum_{k=0,1,2,\dotso} \, m^k = 1, \quad 0 \leq m &lt; 1.
\]
The same formula shows where momentum and gradient accumulation genuinely differ, and why the introduction’s claim that momentum already does accumulation’s job holds only for small \(\lambda\). Accumulation averages the gradients of its micro-batches at one weight, the current one. Momentum averages gradients taken at earlier weights, stale by about one smoothing time, over which the weights move a distance of order \(\lambda\). The difference is exactly that displacement, and it is negligible in the same regime in which the \(O(\lambda)\) corrections of Section 1 can be dropped.
The parameter \(m\) sets how fast those coefficients decay. We want this rate fixed per sample, as in Section 1. The coefficient multiplying each single-sample gradient \(g_i\) should change as little as possible when the batch size changes, which gives \[
    \sqrt[b]{m} = \sqrt[\tilde b]{\tilde m}.
    \label{eq:m-root}
\] So the momentum must be rescaled as \[
    m \rightarrow \tilde m = m^{\frac{\tilde b}{b}}
    \label{eq:m-rescale}
\] to keep the smoothing per sample the same. Figure 6 shows what this means.






Figure 6: Weight coefficients with which each sample gradient \(g_i\) contributes to a training step under the condition \(\eqref{eq:m-root}\). The batch sizes are \(4\) and \(16\), and \(\sqrt[b]{m} = \sqrt[\tilde b]{\tilde m} = 0.97\), so that each sample retains, on average, \(97\%\) of the weight of the previous one, which corresponds to \(m = 0.885\) at \(b = 4\) and \(\tilde m = 0.614\) at \(\tilde b = 16\). The coefficients are grouped into blocks of \(b=4\) or \(\tilde b = 16\), one block per batch. At either batch size the momentum can be tuned so that the coefficients nearly agree. The curve is the same weighting with the batch taken to a single sample, \(w(s) = -\ln r \cdot r^{s}\) with \(r = 0.97\), and each block sits at the average of that curve across it.



The goal of the reparameterization is to separate the technical part, data parallelism, from the real one, the smoothing between gradients. This motivates an alternative parameterization of \(\eqref{eq:sgd-mom-classic}\): \[
    \begin{aligned}
        \mathbf{v}_{n+1} &= e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}}\, \mathbf{v}_{n}
             + (1 - e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}})\, \sum_{i:\, t(i) = n} \mathbf{g}_i(\boldsymbol\theta_n) \\
        \boldsymbol\theta_{n+1} &=
            \boldsymbol\theta_{n} - \lambda\, \mathbf{v}_{n+1}
    \end{aligned},\quad
    \tau \geq 0, \quad
    \lambda &gt; 0.
    \label{eq:sgd-mom-new}
\] The new parameter \(\tau\) is defined as \[
    \tau := - \frac{b}{\ln m^{\text{cl}}},
    \quad \text{equivalently} \quad
    m^{\text{cl}} = e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}},
    \label{eq:tau-def}
\] so that \(e^{-\frac{1}{\tau}} = \sqrt[b]{m^{\text{cl}}}\) is exactly the per-sample decay rate that \(\eqref{eq:m-root}\) asks to be kept fixed. \(\tau\) is the smoothing length measured in samples: each sample gradient loses the factor \(e^{-\frac{1}{\tau}}\) of its weight per elapsed sample, so its contribution decays by \(e\) over \(\tau\) samples regardless of the batch size. We read \(\tau = 0\) as the limit \(\tau \rightarrow 0^{+}\), where \(\eqref{eq:sgd-mom-new}\) degenerates to plain gradient descent, \(\eqref{eq:sgd-classic}\) with \(m^{\text{cl}} = 0\). The larger the value of \(\tau\), the stronger the smoothing.
The advantage is that moving to different hardware does not call for retuning \(\lambda\) and \(\tau\). We set the batch size \(b\) as large as possible, or as large as the stability restrictions of Section 3 allow. Figure 7 confirms this experimentally.






Figure 7: Training loss on MNIST for \(\eqref{eq:sgd-mom-new}\) at \(\lambda = 10^{-3}\). Each curve is the geometric mean over 5 seeds, and the band is their spread. Before each run we warm the velocity up over \(10\,\tau\) samples at \(\lambda = 0\), so the smoothing is already full when the weights start to move. At fixed \(\lambda\) and \(\tau\) the progress per sample does not depend on the batch size, until stability is lost between \(b = 10^{2}\) and \(b = 10^{3}\), where \(\lambda^{\text{cl}} = \lambda b\) leaves the range of Section 3. Those runs stay at chance level or leave the top of the panel. A longer \(\tau\) converges more slowly here, which may differ for other problems.



For \(b \gtrsim \tau\), however, the momentum smoothing is weak compared with the batch smoothing, and the approximation of Figure 6 no longer holds.
The relations between the proposed and the classic normalization are: \[
\begin{aligned}
    m^{\text{cl}} &= e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}} \\
    \lambda^{\text{cl}} &= \left(1 - e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}} \right) \lambda\, b
\end{aligned}
\]
A rule of this kind is already in use. Smith, Kindermans, Ying and Le characterize momentum gradient descent by what they call the noise scale: the coefficient of the noise correlation in a Langevin equation of the kind Section 2 discusses, and a temperature in the sense used there. They prescribe holding it fixed when the batch size is changed. Their learning rate, batch size and momentum coefficient are \(\lambda^{\text{cl}}\), \(b\) and \(m^{\text{cl}}\) here, so with \(N\) the size of the training set their quantity reads \[
    \text{noise scale}
        := \frac{\lambda^{\text{cl}}\, N}{b \left(1 - m^{\text{cl}}\right)}
         = \frac{\left(1 - e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}}\right) \lambda\, b\, N}
                {b \left(1 - e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}}\right)}
         = \lambda\, N ,
\] where the middle expression substitutes the two relations above. The factor \(N\) is theirs, carried by a cost summed over the training examples rather than averaged over them. So their prescription is the instruction to keep \(\lambda\) fixed, and neither the batch size nor the momentum coefficient enters it on its own.
That leaves \(\tau\) free, which is where the two jobs separate. Holding \(\lambda\) fixed says nothing about the smoothing, and keeping the smoothing fixed as well is the extra demand of \(\eqref{eq:m-rescale}\): raising the batch by a factor \(k\) asks for \(m^{\text{cl}} \to \left(m^{\text{cl}}\right)^{k}\), a momentum coefficient moving down. The cited authors move it up instead, on purpose, since their aim is to spend fewer updates rather than to keep the trajectory. They report the price: a slight loss of test accuracy, which they attribute to the averaging timescale growing until the average can no longer follow the training. They count that timescale in epochs; multiplied by the number of samples in an epoch it becomes \[
    \text{averaging timescale}
        := \frac{b}{1 - m^{\text{cl}}}
         = \tau \left(1 + O\!\left(\frac{b}{\tau}\right)\right)
\] samples, so what they are describing is a change of \(\tau\).
The effect of momentum itself is of the same order as the effect of the batch size, and the two come from the same place. A gradient enters the update at a weight the run has already left: by up to \(b\) samples inside a batch, and by about \(\tau\) samples under the smoothing. Both corrections are of first order in \(\lambda\) with those lengths multiplying it, so at a small enough \(\lambda\) neither one is visible.
Yuan, Ying and Sayed proved this in the classical variables: momentum gradient descent with a small constant step size follows the trajectory of the plain method run at the step size \[
    \frac{\lambda^{\text{cl}}}{1 - m^{\text{cl}}}
        = \frac{\left(1 - e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}}\right) \lambda\, b}
               {1 - e^{-\frac{b}{\tau}}}
         = \lambda\, b ,
\] which is the classical learning rate of plain gradient descent at the same per-sample rate, \(\eqref{eq:lambda-cl}\). Their rescaling therefore asks for nothing in the per-sample variables, and \(\tau\) leaves the leading order. Li, Tai and E reach the same point from their stochastic equation for momentum, where the velocity relaxes over \(\tau\) samples and then follows the gradient. The same combination carries the momentum in the noise scale of Smith and Le. Kidambi et al. go further and find no advantage in momentum on stochastic gradients at all, reading the gains seen in practice as a by-product of mini-batching.
We take the effects of momentum no further. The smoothing and the batch size differ in the length of the window they average over, then, and not in the order of magnitude in \(\lambda\) at which they enter, and this section needs only that the smoothing has a parameter of its own. Without momentum there is no such parameter and the smoothing simply goes undone. The batch size does not take it over: by Section 1 and Section 2 a larger batch buys a cleaner gradient per step and proportionally fewer steps, which leaves the noise over a budget of samples where it was.
